CDN network/content delivery network: improves read performance, content is cached at the edge. DDOS protections

origins: backend where you can connect to

S3 bucket: distributing files and caching them at edge, uploading files, securing OAC/Orgin access control
VPC orgin: application hosted  in VPC private subnets
Custom origin/HTTP: S3 website or any public HTTP backend you want

how it works:
client -> cloudfront edge location or local cache-> orgin

vpc origins are used to connect alb or ec2

geo restriction:
  1) allowlist: default denies users to access the content unless you allow them
  2) blocklist: default allows users to access the content unless your block them 

cache invalidations: happens when you change back-end origin. Because update happens only after TTL expired.

unicast IP: one server holds one IP address

anycast IP: all servers hold the same IP address

AWS global accelerator: uses 2 anycast IP addresss. Where the closest edge locations is used by the user.
only difference between aws global accelerator and cloudfront is that global accelerator loads it's content from the orgin to the edge device that's closest to the user.
 


